  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is near zero (0.00-0.20) and recent history shows high volatility or a spike, the model systematically underestimates the magnitude of the subsequent crash; apply a downward correction factor of 15-25% to the forecasted decline.",
    "The model exhibits severe mean reversion bias in Example 3/4 where it predicts a slow linear recovery after a crash, while true outcomes show sharp V-shaped rebounds (e.g., from ~67 to ~80) within 10 days; increase forecast volatility and add a convexity adjustment for the rebound phase.",
    "In high-sentiment or neutral regimes with prior spikes (Examples 1/2), the model fails to capture the 'mean reversion' effect where prices revert to historical averages after extreme moves, resulting in an upward bias during the recovery phase; reduce the forecasted upside by approximately 5-8%.",
    "The model consistently underestimates the speed of price movement during crisis events (Examples 3/4), predicting gradual trends instead of rapid swings; apply a time-decompression correction to accelerate the forecast trajectory for the first 10 days post-event."
  ]
}
